   "slug": "robert-earl-filner",
   "months": 2,
   "why": "He refused to post bond after the June 16, 1961 arrest and stayed in for about two months. That is a sourced duration with no recorded endpoints, so it is stored as months and anchored on the arrest.",
   "note": "\n\nHe refused to post bond and remained in custody for approximately two months. The charges are recorded as disturbing the peace and inciting a riot. The institutional sequence and the release date are not established, so the two months is recorded as a duration rather than as a pair of dates."
  }
 ],
 "prose_durations": [
  {
   "slug": "byron-mark-baer",
   "marker": "45 days in Mississippi custody",
   "note": "\n\nHe spent 45 days in Mississippi custody after the July 29, 1961 arrest, including imprisonment at the Mississippi State Penitentiary at Parchman, where Rick Sheviakov was one of his cellmates. His prison correspondence survives in the archive, along with the communication codes the riders used and a letter written on toilet paper.\n\nNo imprisonment figure is published for him. The admission and discharge dates are unresolved, and 45 days is neither a pair of dates nor a whole number of months — the two forms this archive stores. Rounding it to one month or two would publish a figure the source does not support, so the 45 days stays here in the text where it is exact."
  },
  {
   "slug": "rick-stanley-ogilvie-sheviakov",
   "marker": "nearly 45 days at Parchman",
   "note": "\n\nHe spent nearly 45 days at the Mississippi State Penitentiary at Parchman after the arrest at the Jackson Greyhound station, with Byron Baer among his cellmates. His identification and Parchman correspondence are held by the Mississippi Department of Archives and History.\n\nThe arrest date is not settled. The catalog title says July 21, 1961 while the photograph's date-of-original field says July 29; the reading on record is that July 21 is the arrest and July 29 the processing of the image. This record keeps July 29 as it was entered and notes the conflict rather than silently moving it.\n\nAs with Byron Baer, no imprisonment figure is published: 45 days is neither a pair of dates nor a whole number of months, so it is recorded here in the text where it is exact."
  }
 ],
